  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Isolated shower and thunderstorm chances continue today, with\nincreasing severe threat on Friday and Saturday. \n\nThe large scale pattern continues to remain largely unchanged\nfrom earlier in the week, with upper level ridging remaining\nquasi-stationary across the western Conus, and upper level\ntroughing across the Northeast. At the surface, a stationary\nfront will generally remain within the vicinity of the Virginia \nand North Carolina border through at least the weekend, which \nwill allow for daily chances of showers and thunderstorms. With \nthe upper level pattern mentioned above, there continues to be a\nmarginal threat for Mesoscale convective systems to enter the \narea along this stationary front out of the Ohio valley through \nthe weekend. \n\nThere continues to be some uncertainty in the severity of these\nsystems as they approach the area, as timing of them entering\nthe area will determine how severe they will be. If these\nsystems approach during the afternoon and evening hours, with a\npeak unstable environment over the region, then increased\nchances for severe winds will be possible; however, if these\nsystems approach overnight or during the early morning hours,\nthe chances for severe winds diminish significantly. \n\nBy late Friday, the upper level ridge also starts to build over\nthe central Gulf states, which will nudge the train tracks so to\nspeak for these MCS further north and east of the region during\nthe weekend. Overall, the chance for showers and thunderstorms\nlooks to continue for the next several days, with today\nremaining limited in coverage for much of the region. \n\nTemperatures through the period will likely stay above normal \nexcept a brief drop to near normal this weekend.",
